{
  "version": 1,
  "summary": "Closes false-green paths: an unrun selected claim in a green claim run, weak intents in every product shape, and near-miss doc anchors; refuses an empty intent.",
  "files": [
    {
      "path": "internal/cli/surface.go",
      "summary": "attachCoverage owns the rule that a green claim run proved every selected claim.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [153, 156],
          "summary": "attachCoverage turns a green or unset run verdict into inconclusive when a selected claim has no proving step.",
          "rationale": "Issue #48: a planner bug made run --claim exit 0 green while its claim line said not run. A red stays red because a red is backed by evidence. The run record is saved after this, and the usage entry reads the record in a deferred call, so every view says inconclusive. No CLI route reaches the state since #47, so TestAClaimRunWithAnUnrunSelectedClaimIsNotGreen forces it in package cli; with the guard off it prints exit 0 green.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-false-greens"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/index/index.go",
      "summary": "The intent lookup now blocks weak intents in a one-claim product and where a claim lacks the shared nouns.",
      "annotations": [
        {
          "newRange": [269, 276],
          "summary": "Intent returns nothing when the claims found leave an intent term uncovered and the intent names none of them.",
          "rationale": "The zero-trust review (D3, D4) showed store item refunds still went green with one claim or with a fourth claim that lacks item, because the common-term rule only works when a noun is in every claim. An uncovered term is a filler word or the weak intent's subject, and only a named claim tells them apart. Measured over 56 intents on 4 product shapes: every test, docs and verify-verilex intent keeps its claims in its real product; the scratch fourth-claim product loses two apple intents (a false red).",
          "author": "fm/verilex-false-greens"
        },
        {
          "newRange": [296, 303],
          "summary": "names accepts all non-common name terms, or one name term that at most half the claims hold.",
          "rationale": "The rare-term clause keeps long intents such as CI scripts resolve origin/main from the branch ref only, where the placeholder rule drops CI and origin/main is one token. The two-term rule in covers still uses only the full-name clause, so refused payment keeps finding nothing.",
          "author": "fm/verilex-false-greens"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "path": "internal/index/docs.go",
      "summary": "A comment that opens with the word claim or claims is an anchor unless it goes on with a plain word that is no claim id.",
      "annotations": [
